## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates expertise in online learning, latency budgeting, drift handling, and model calibration within a regulated financial environment.

## Key Concepts
- Online learning and ensemble methods (streaming gradient boosting, Hoeffding trees)
- Concept drift detection and rolling retraining cadence
- Calibration under real-time constraints
- Explainability and regulatory auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Online SGD update for logistic regression
let w = initialize();
for (const x of stream) {
  const yPred = sigmoid(dot(w, x.features));
  const error = yPred - x.label;
  w = w.subtract(gradient(error, x.features).scale(learningRate));
}
```

## Design Considerations
1. **Feature Engineering**: Structured features (transaction amount, merchant category, geography) combined with rolling window statistics (velocity, average amounts, deviation from historical baseline)
2. **Model Selection**: Hoeffding trees offer memory-bounded growth; streaming GBM provides better accuracy with incremental leaf updates
3. **Calibration**: Apply isotonic regression or Platt scaling on a calibration window to ensure probability outputs are well-calibrated
4. **Drift Detection**: Monitor PSI or KL divergence on feature distributions; trigger retraining when thresholds exceed predefined limits
5. **Latency Budget**: Feature computation (2–5 ms) + model inference (1–3 ms) + serialization/deserialization (1–2 ms) = total under 15 ms

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests building a scalable, privacy-preserving, multi-tenant ML platform with end-to-end governance, not just a single model. It emphasizes per-tenant isolation, drift monitoring, fairness constraints, and safe rollout strategies in production.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant isolation and data governance
- Privacy-preserving techniques (DP/FL)
- Drift detection and alerting
- Fairness and per-tenant constraints
- Offline/online evaluation and canary rollouts
- Observability and rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
function driftScore(distA, distB) {
  // simplistic KL-divergence proxy
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < distA.length; i++) {
    const a = distA[i] + 1e-9, b = distB[i] + 1e-9;
    s += a * Math.log(a / b);
  }
  return s;
}
```

